  "craters_missing": [],
  "description": "A patch centred on each benchmark crater is the query. It ranks every regular 3.84 km grid patch from the other regions. A grid patch counts as a hit when its window fully contains another benchmark crater's rim (|dx| and |dy| at most 1.92 km minus half the crater diameter); grid patches within 5 km of a crater that do not contain it are left out. Positives per crater: Barringer (Meteor Crater) 1, New Quebec (Pingualuit) 0, Lonar 1, Roter Kamm 0, Tenoumer 1, Tswaing (formerly Pretoria Saltpan) 4, Wolfe Creek 4. Craters with no positive (rim wider than the window allows at the grid offset) still act as queries.",
  "index": "Index: all valid Earth grid patches (land-cover masks off).",
  "combined_note": "The combined row uses the Earth-to-Earth sigma (0.163), at which the terrain term is close to zero for most crater pairs, so it tracks the embedding. For Moon targets sigma is 0.44-0.71 and the terrain term carries real weight, so this row is not the exact ranker the app uses for them.",
  "finding": "At 60 m the 20 interpretable terrain features alone recognise 1-3 km craters only weakly (AUC 0.63, about the same as relief alone at 0.63, random 0.55); the learned image embedding does much better (AUC 0.98, another crater in the top 10 for 86% of queries). The app therefore uses both: feature values to explain a match in physical units, and the embedding to rank landform shape.",
  "revision_note": "An earlier version of this test also used the crater-centred patches as the relevant candidates. The embedding then ranked another crater first for every query (mean reciprocal rank 1.00, AUC 0.979), an artefact of exact centring that a real search never has. Candidates are now regular grid patches only; the positive rule above was fixed from geometry before this run.",
